---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { REVIEW_LABEL } from "../lib/catalog";
---

<BaseLayout title="About — Awesome RGBDT Manipulation">
  <article class="prose wrap">
    <a class="back-link" href="/">
      ← Back to the library
    </a>
    <h1>A clearer view of contact-rich manipulation.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      An independent, source-backed reference for robotic manipulation
      benchmarks and policies, spanning RGB, depth, tactile sensing and force
      feedback.
    </p>
    <h2>Two ways into the literature</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Benchmarks</strong> describe what is tested: published tasks,
      evaluation domains, simulator versions, sensors, robot embodiments,
      demonstrations, metrics and protocols. Task figures show the suite or
      capability taxonomy whenever an appropriate author figure is available.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Policies</strong> describe how actions are produced: architecture,
      base model, deployment inputs, action outputs, model size, learning data,
      training resources and inference or control rates. Their figures show the
      method pipeline or architecture.
    </p>
    <p>
      A paper can contribute to both collections. It keeps one canonical
      research record, with collection-specific details and figures. Supporting
      datasets, tactile simulators and perception tests are labeled separately
      from closed-loop manipulation benchmarks.
    </p>
    <h2>Evidence before completeness claims</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Primary papers, author project pages, official repositories and model
        cards support the records. Secondary lists are discovery aids.
      </li>
      <li>
        Publication dates use the first public paper version where verified. An
        anonymous, undated project stays undated.
      </li>
      <li>
        Paper experiments and current software releases can use different
        simulators, datasets or checkpoints. Both scopes are retained.
      </li>
      <li>
        Undisclosed values stay <em>Not reported</em>. Unresolved values stay{" "}
        <em>Not verified</em>. Neither means zero, absent or closed source.
      </li>
      <li>
        Code, model weights and data have separate release and licensing fields.
        A project link does not establish an open release.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Physical observations and deployment inputs</h2>
    <p>
      Rendered ground-truth depth, reconstructed sensor depth, geometry
      predicted from RGB, tactile images, taxel force arrays and wrist
      force/torque are distinct observations. Privileged simulator state or
      tactile labels used during training are not automatically deployment
      inputs. Policy details describe these differences explicitly.
    </p>
    <p>
      Model sizes identify the component when reported. A training GPU count,
      inference device, action throughput, environment step rate and closed-loop
      control frequency are different measurements. This atlas retains their
      source scope rather than turning them into a shared performance ranking.
    </p>
    <h2>Figures and attribution</h2>
    <p>
      Research figures come from author publications, projects or official
      repositories. Images are optimized locally and retain their source URL,
      author credit and original rights. Click a figure to enlarge it and follow
      its source. Missing figures are stated explicitly.
    </p>
    <h2>Keeping the atlas current</h2>
    <p>
      This is a reviewed snapshot dated <strong>{REVIEW_LABEL}</strong>, not a
      live publication feed. The <a href="/coverage/">coverage page</a>{" "}
      describes the search boundary and unresolved fields. The local repository
      includes structured data, regeneration and validation scripts, and a
      review workflow for future additions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Search accepts task names, simulators, model names, sensors and training
      details. Filters are collection-specific. Save papers in this browser,
      compare up to four records, export a filtered CSV, or share a URL that
      restores the current view.
    </p>
    <div class="notice">
      Original repository code and prose use the MIT license. Third-party
      research figures, linked code, datasets and model weights retain their
      respective licenses and rights.
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
